const _200DisallowInRobotstxtExplanationNeeded = "---\nimport BlogPost from \"../../layouts/BlogPost.astro\";\n\nconst title = \"Disallow: /*? in robots.txt explanation needed\";\nconst description = \"Disallow: /*? in robots.txt Explained: Crawling Dynamic URLs Understanding how robots.txt directives interact with dynamic URLs, especially those utilizing...\";\nconst date = \"2026-08-10\";\n---\n\n<BlogPost\n  title={title}\n  description={description}\n  date={date}\n>\n  <h1>Disallow: /*? in robots.txt Explained: Crawling Dynamic URLs</h1>\n  <p>Understanding how <code>robots.txt</code> directives interact with dynamic URLs, especially those utilizing query parameters, is a common point of confusion for developers and SEO practitioners. When you set rules like <code>Disallow: /*?</code>, you are defining constraints on how web crawlers (like Googlebot) access your site. This explanation will break down exactly what this rule means for crawling dynamic links, particularly in e-commerce scenarios, and what you need to consider when structuring your site architecture.</p>\n  <h2>The Mechanics of Robots.txt Wildcards</h2>\n  <p>The <code>robots.txt</code> file uses specific syntax to define rules for crawlers. Directives like <code>Disallow</code> use wildcards (<code>*</code>) to match patterns. The wildcard matches zero or more characters.</p>\n  <p>When you use <code>Disallow: /*?</code>, you are instructing the crawler to disallow access to any URL path that contains a question mark (<code>?</code>). This pattern targets URLs that utilize query strings, which are typically used to pass dynamic data to a server—such as search queries, sorting parameters, or filtering options.</p>\n  <p>The crucial point here is that <code>robots.txt</code> controls <em>crawling</em>, not <em>indexing</em>. If Googlebot cannot crawl a page (because it's disallowed), it cannot read the content or evaluate its relevance for indexing purposes. However, if you are using this rule to manage SEO implications regarding dynamic content, understanding the distinction between crawling and indexing is vital.</p>\n  <h2>Impact on Dynamic E-commerce Links</h2>\n  <p>Let’s examine your specific examples:\n  1. <code>https://www.whateverwebsite.de/search/?q=hello</code>\n  2. <code>https://www.whateverwebsite.de/category.html?form_new=18658</code>\n  3. <code>https://www.whateverwebsite.de/search/?q=grand&amp;productFilter=motive_design%3AGeometric</code></p>\n  <p>Because these URLs all contain a <code>?</code>, they match the pattern specified by <code>Disallow: /*?</code>. Therefore, under this rule, the crawler would be instructed to <em>not</em> crawl these specific dynamic search and filtering links.</p>\n  <p>This is often desirable if you are trying to prevent crawlers from wasting crawl budget on parameter-heavy URLs that don't represent unique, indexable content, or if those parameters contain sensitive session data.</p>\n  <h2>Best Practices for Dynamic Content Handling</h2>\n  <p>While blocking crawling via <code>robots.txt</code> is possible, it’s important to understand the broader context of how modern applications handle dynamic routing and SEO. For complex e-commerce sites, simply blocking crawlers might not be the most effective long-term strategy.</p>\n  <p>A more robust approach involves using server-side redirection or canonical tags when dealing with parameter-heavy URLs. Instead of relying solely on <code>robots.txt</code> to block access:</p>\n  <ol>\n  <li><strong>Implement Server Redirection:</strong> Use 301 redirects to point parameter-heavy search results back to clean, indexable category pages if appropriate, ensuring that link equity is passed correctly without blocking the crawler entirely.</li>\n  <li><strong>Use <code>noindex</code> Meta Tag:</strong> If you want Google to crawl a page but ensure it doesn't appear in search results (e.g., internal admin or filtered result pages), using the <code>noindex</code> meta tag within the HTML <code>&lt;head&gt;</code> is more direct than relying on <code>robots.txt</code>.</li>\n  <li><strong>Leveraging Framework Capabilities:</strong> When building dynamic applications, understanding how routing works within a framework like Laravel is essential. For instance, in Laravel, you manage routing and view generation; ensuring that your application structure cleanly separates public-facing content from internal parameters helps maintain SEO health. As we build powerful systems using frameworks like Laravel, managing these intricate data flows becomes even more important to get the most out of your site’s potential.</li>\n  </ol>\n  <p>By combining careful use of <code>robots.txt</code> for broad structural control with proper server-side handling and HTML meta tags, you gain much finer control over how search engines interact with your dynamic content.</p>\n\n</BlogPost>\n";

